# Three.js Scroll World — Page-Native 3D Storytelling

Use this tier when the brief explicitly calls for a fly-through, spatial journey, explorable system, or product story that benefits from moving a camera through one continuous world. The world replaces the page's signature scene; it is not an effect added to an ordinary landing page.

## Contents

1. Core contract
2. Beat ledger
3. Page-scroll architecture
4. React/R3F implementation
5. Vanilla Three.js implementation
6. Performance and lifecycle
7. Art direction
8. Mobile and fallback behavior
9. Debug checklist

## 1. Core contract

- Use one page scroll context, one scene, one perspective camera, one renderer, and one world group.
- Let the browser own wheel, touch, keyboard, scrollbar, and history behavior. Never call `preventDefault` to own scrolling.
- Convert page position to a normalized progress value from 0 to 1. That value drives camera position/target, object state, light, and DOM beat selection.
- Make every state deterministic from progress so scrolling upward perfectly reverses the story.
- Keep headings, body copy, links, and CTAs in semantic DOM above the canvas.
- Mount the renderer only near the section, unmount or pause it offscreen, handle context loss, and release GPU resources.
- Replace the world with a complete static poster when WebGL is unavailable or reduced motion is requested.

Avoid nested `ScrollControls` for a full-page story. A second scroll container breaks keyboard/page scrolling, URL positioning, and the single-source-of-truth contract. It is acceptable only for a deliberately self-contained embedded 3D widget.

## 2. Beat ledger

Write five to seven keyframes before rendering code:

```js
export const beats = [
  { id: "hook", at: 0.00, camPos: [0, 2, 8], camTarget: [0, 0, 0], title: "Enter the archive" },
  { id: "origin", at: 0.22, camPos: [3, 1, 5], camTarget: [2, 0, -2], title: "Where it began" },
  { id: "system", at: 0.46, camPos: [0, 4, 2], camTarget: [0, 0, -4], title: "The engine underneath" },
  { id: "proof", at: 0.72, camPos: [-3, 1, 5], camTarget: [-2, 0, -2], title: "Built for scale" },
  { id: "cta", at: 1.00, camPos: [0, 1, 4], camTarget: [0, 0, 0], title: "Begin the journey" },
];
```

Ledger rules:

1. `at` values increase from exactly 0 to exactly 1.
2. Interpolate between the surrounding keyframes with one smooth easing function.
3. Use one dominant metaphor and one dominant object per beat.
4. Put the H1 in the opening DOM beat and the primary CTA in the closing beat.
5. Place pricing, forms, legal content, and the footer in ordinary DOM after the world.
6. Use approximately one viewport of scroll per beat, then tune for reading—not spectacle.

## 3. Page-scroll architecture

Use a tall relative wrapper, a sticky canvas, and sequential DOM overlays:

```html
<section id="world" style="position:relative;height:500vh">
  <div class="world-canvas" style="position:sticky;top:0;height:100vh"></div>
  <div class="world-copy" style="position:absolute;inset:0">
    <section style="height:100vh">…opening beat…</section>
    <section style="height:100vh">…origin beat…</section>
    <section style="height:100vh">…system beat…</section>
    <section style="height:100vh">…proof beat…</section>
    <section style="height:100vh">…CTA beat…</section>
  </div>
</section>
```

Calculate progress in a passive scroll listener coordinated through one animation frame:

```js
const rect = wrapper.getBoundingClientRect();
const distance = Math.max(1, rect.height - innerHeight);
const progress = Math.min(1, Math.max(0, -rect.top / distance));
```

Do not read layout in the render loop. Update the progress ref on scroll/resize, then let rendering consume the ref.

## 4. React/R3F implementation

Use `assets/ScrollWorld3D.jsx` as the full reference. Its load-bearing decisions are:

- `progressRef.current` is updated from page scroll, not an internal scroll container;
- `useFrame` reads the ref and interpolates camera keyframes without React state churn;
- the wrapper's IntersectionObserver mounts `<Canvas>` only near/in the world and unmounts it after exit;
- desktop DPR is capped at 2 and mobile at 1.5;
- a WebGL context-loss listener swaps to the poster;
- DOM beats occupy the same tall wrapper and remain SEO/accessibility-visible;
- R3F disposes scene resources when Canvas unmounts.

Install only when the project does not already provide compatible packages:

```bash
npm install three @react-three/fiber
```

Do not add drei or GSAP solely for this architecture; the reference uses neither. If the project already has a suitable scroll/progress utility, feed its normalized page progress into the same ref contract.

## 5. Vanilla Three.js implementation

Use the same architecture with explicit lifecycle management:

```js
import * as THREE from "three";

export function mountWorld({ wrapper, canvas, beats, showPoster }) {
  const reduced = matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)");
  if (reduced.matches || !canvas.getContext("webgl")) {
    showPoster();
    return () => {};
  }

  const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ canvas, antialias: true, alpha: false });
  const scene = new THREE.Scene();
  const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(45, 1, 0.1, 100);
  const world = new THREE.Group();
  scene.add(world);

  const hero = new THREE.Mesh(
    new THREE.IcosahedronGeometry(1.4, 1),
    new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x8b5cf6, roughness: 0.25, metalness: 0.65, flatShading: true }),
  );
  world.add(hero);
  scene.add(new THREE.AmbientLight(0xffffff, 0.4));
  const key = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 1.2);
  key.position.set(4, 6, 4);
  scene.add(key);

  let progress = 0;
  let frameId = 0;
  let scrollFrameId = 0;
  let running = false;

  const resize = () => {
    const width = canvas.clientWidth;
    const height = canvas.clientHeight;
    renderer.setPixelRatio(Math.min(devicePixelRatio || 1, innerWidth < 768 ? 1.5 : 2));
    renderer.setSize(width, height, false);
    camera.aspect = width / Math.max(1, height);
    camera.updateProjectionMatrix();
  };

  const updateProgress = () => {
    scrollFrameId = 0;
    const rect = wrapper.getBoundingClientRect();
    progress = Math.min(1, Math.max(0, -rect.top / Math.max(1, rect.height - innerHeight)));
  };

  const onScroll = () => {
    if (!scrollFrameId) scrollFrameId = requestAnimationFrame(updateProgress);
  };

  const render = () => {
    if (!running) return;
    applyBeatCamera(camera, beats, progress); // deterministic interpolation helper
    hero.rotation.y = progress * Math.PI;
    renderer.render(scene, camera);
    frameId = requestAnimationFrame(render);
  };

  const observer = new IntersectionObserver(([entry]) => {
    if (entry.isIntersecting && !running) {
      running = true;
      frameId = requestAnimationFrame(render);
    } else if (!entry.isIntersecting) {
      running = false;
      cancelAnimationFrame(frameId);
    }
  }, { rootMargin: "25%" });

  const onContextLost = (event) => {
    event.preventDefault();
    showPoster();
  };

  resize();
  updateProgress();
  observer.observe(wrapper);
  addEventListener("scroll", onScroll, { passive: true });
  addEventListener("resize", resize, { passive: true });
  canvas.addEventListener("webglcontextlost", onContextLost);

  return () => {
    running = false;
    cancelAnimationFrame(frameId);
    cancelAnimationFrame(scrollFrameId);
    observer.disconnect();
    removeEventListener("scroll", onScroll);
    removeEventListener("resize", resize);
    canvas.removeEventListener("webglcontextlost", onContextLost);
    scene.traverse((object) => {
      object.geometry?.dispose();
      const materials = Array.isArray(object.material) ? object.material : [object.material];
      materials.forEach((material) => {
        material?.map?.dispose();
        material?.dispose();
      });
    });
    renderer.dispose();
  };
}
```

Implement `applyBeatCamera` with reusable vectors allocated outside the render loop, as shown in the React asset. If a scene uses shared textures/materials, dispose each resource once.

## 6. Performance and lifecycle

- Keep DPR ≤2 desktop and ≤1.5 mobile.
- Use primitives, low segment counts, fog, and three or fewer lights.
- Avoid real-time shadows unless one bounded 1024 map materially improves the scene.
- Compress textures and models; do not load 4K textures for mobile-sized objects.
- Allocate vectors, colors, and curves outside `useFrame`/render loops.
- Pause or unmount offscreen; pause when the document is hidden.
- Remove every listener/observer and dispose geometry, material, texture, render target, and renderer.
- Test repeated route entry/exit for memory growth.

## 7. Art direction

Create three quick studies before implementation. Vary metaphor, field/background, material, light behavior, camera relationship, and typography placement. Reject generic glowing planets and random floating cubes unless the product story specifically supports them.

Derive DOM and 3D colors from one theme family. Keep text sovereign: use a stable quiet region, scrim, or glass panel and verify contrast over the busiest frame.

## 8. Mobile and fallback behavior

- Tighten camera arcs for tall screens and keep the focal object inside safe copy zones.
- Reduce geometry, effects, and lights; remove shadows.
- Keep touch scrolling native and avoid hover-only meaning.
- Swap to the poster on reduced motion, WebGL failure/context loss, or a project-defined low-power path.
- The poster contains the real H1, supporting copy, and primary CTA; it is not a blank canvas screenshot.

## 9. Debug checklist

- Story does not reverse exactly: remove time-driven state and derive it from progress.
- Camera is frozen: confirm page progress changes and no nested scroller owns input.
- Jank: inspect DPR, shadow maps, texture size, draw calls, layout reads, and garbage allocation.
- Text is unreadable: adjust scene composition/scrim rather than lowering text contrast.
- Blank canvas: test capability detection, context loss, import paths, and the poster path.
- Memory grows after navigation: verify Canvas unmount or explicit disposal and listener cleanup.
